<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
firewalld service definition for Maze Connect.

Both ports carry the same number so there is one to remember:
* TCP 38271 — the mutual-TLS link. Everything meaningful happens here,
and every byte of it is authenticated and pinned before this
application sees it.
* UDP 38271 — the discovery beacon. Unauthenticated and informational
only; it populates a "nearby devices" list and nothing in it is acted
on before pairing completes.

Opening these does not widen the trust model. An unpaired peer that
reaches the TCP port can send pairing messages and nothing else, and
pairing still requires a six-digit code to be compared by a human on both
screens. What the rule fixes is the opposite failure: a firewall silently
dropping the link so that two devices which have already been paired
simply never see each other.

Installing this file only *defines* the service. Adding it to a zone is
done by the package's install scriptlet.
-->
<service>
  <short>Maze Connect</short>
  <description>Device link for Maze Connect: mutual-TLS connections on TCP 38271 and LAN discovery beacons on UDP 38271. Intended for a home or otherwise trusted network.</description>
  <port protocol="tcp" port="38271"/>
  <port protocol="udp" port="38271"/>
</service>
